fetchconfig -- Developer Guide
==============================

This guide documents how fetchconfig works internally, the data structures it
uses, the device-table format, the security mechanisms in place, and how to
extend it. It is written for an engineer who needs to add functionality.

It complements, and does not replace, the following existing documents, which
remain authoritative for their topics and are referenced from here:

- README -- the user manual (installation, every option, email, the
  directory allowlist, command-line reference).
- How-To-add-a-model -- the step-by-step recipe for a new device model.
- README.generic-model -- the template-driven generic model.
- README.template_engine -- the template grammar and parser internals.
- README.record-session -- tools/record-session.pl, used to capture a
  device session when writing a new model.
- DEPENDENCIES -- the Perl module dependency matrix.

Where this guide found those documents lacking a detail an extender needs,
that detail is added here and noted as such.

Target version: 9.73. Perl floor: 5.26.

------------------------------------------------------------

1. What fetchconfig is
======================

fetchconfig is a command-line tool (not a daemon) that logs in to network
devices, retrieves their running configuration, and stores a timestamped copy
in a per-device directory, keeping only the configs that differ from the
previous run. It is normally invoked from cron. A separate project,
fetchconfig-web, is a CGI front-end that reads the stored backups and can
invoke fetchconfig to run a backup on demand.

Key properties that shape the whole design:

- One shot per invocation. fetchconfig parses its device table, fetches,
  writes, mails, and exits. It holds no state between runs except what is on
  disk (the backups, .status files, lock files).
- Models never terminate the run. A device that fails to fetch is
  recorded as failed and the run continues to the next device. Only
  configuration errors in the device table or the directory allowlist abort
  before fetching.
- The device table is the trust boundary. Whoever can write the device
  table supplies every device credential and can cause code execution via
  on_fetch_run. See section 9 (Security).

