2. Architecture and execution model
===================================

2.1 Module map
--------------

File                           Role
-----------------------------  -------------------------------------------------
fetchconfig.pl                 Driver: CLI option parsing, device-table loading,
                               dispatch to a maintenance/lookup mode or the
                               fetch phase, the parallel-fetch (-P) fork loop,
                               final exit code.
fetchconfig/model/Detector.pm  The registry and the per-device pipeline. Holds
                               the in-memory device table, parses
                               default:/email:/directory:/device lines, enforces
                               the directory allowlist, and runs fetch_device()
                               per device.
fetchconfig/model/Abstract.pm  The base class for every model. Provides the
                               shared API: SSH/telnet session helpers, prompt
                               handling, backup write/compare/expire, option
                               accessors, secret handling, the debug-file
                               helpers, locking primitives.
fetchconfig/model/*.pm         The device models (34 device models +
                               AbstractHTTP base +
                               GenericTemplate/GenericTemplateParser). Each
                               implements do_fetch() for its transport/CLI
                               dialect.
fetchconfig/Tools.pm           The maintenance/lookup modes (-g, -l, -z/-Z, -o,
                               -e, -s/-S, --list-allowed-dirs, --clean-lock-dir,
                               etc.) and their shared filesystem helpers.
fetchconfig/Mailer.pm          SMTP delivery and the result record:
                               record_result(), the per-recipient grouping, the
                               HTML + plain-text summary mail.
fetchconfig/Report.pm          The on-disk HTML change report and the
                               config-diff masking (mask_line).
fetchconfig/Constants.pm       version() and a few constants.
fetchconfig/Logger.pm          The logger object (prefix, levels:
                               debug/info/error).

2.2 The two phases
------------------

fetchconfig runs in two clearly separated phases. Keeping them separate is a
deliberate design rule -- understand it before adding per-device behaviour.

Phase 1 -- parse and register (no fetching).
fetchconfig.pl loads every -devices= file and every -line= argument and
calls Detector::parse() on each line. parse():

1. recognises the line type (email:, directory:, default:, or a device
   line -- see section 4),
2. for a device line, resolves the model, merges default: options with the
   device's own options into a fresh $dev_opt_tab, validates (dev_id
   pattern, numeric keep), and records the device in %dev_info_table +
   @dev_order.

No device is contacted in phase 1. The lookup modes (-g, -l, ...) use
exactly this registration and then operate on the stored backups without ever
entering phase 2.

Phase 2 -- fetch.
After phase 1, fetchconfig.pl either dispatches to a maintenance/lookup
mode and exits, or calls run_fetch_phase(), which iterates @dev_order and
calls Detector::fetch_device($dev_id) for each -- sequentially, or in forked
workers under -P (section 2.4).

2.3 The per-device pipeline (fetch_device)
------------------------------------------

For each device, fetch_device() performs, in order:

1. Resolve options needed up front: changes_only, on_fetch_run,
   on_fetch_cat, and whether a previous backup must be located.
2. Locate previous backup (only when changes_only, on_fetch_run or
   on_fetch_cat needs it): find_latest scans the repository for the most
   recent prior backup, by parsed timestamp; the result feeds the compare in
   step 8. A freshly added device has none -- that normal first-run state is
   logged at info, inside the device's block. This runs before the guards
   below, so the scan happens even for a device a later guard will skip.
3. System-directory check (always): reject a repository=,
   template_dir= or on_fetch_run program that points into a system
   directory; skip the device if so (section 9).
4. No-allowlist warning (if no directory: section): warn once per run
   per distinct unrestricted directory.
5. Directory-allowlist enforcement (only if a directory: section
   exists): repository=/template_dir= must resolve to an allowed path;
   on_fetch_run's program directory must be allowed; a $TAG is expanded
   to its real path. Off-list -> skip the device.
6. Lock (if lock=on): acquire a per-device flock; on contention,
   retry, then record skipped and return (section 8).
7. Fetch: $mod->fetch(...) -> the model's do_fetch() logs in, runs the
   show-config command, and returns the written backup path (or undef on
   failure). On debug=on, the session is captured to <dev_id>.debug.
8. Compare: compare the just-written config against the previous latest
   backup located in step 2 (config_equal, ignoring volatile lines). If
   unchanged, discard the just-written file (config_discard) so only
   changes are kept.
9. Expire: purge_ancient deletes the oldest backups beyond keep=N.
10. Record: Mailer::record_result(...) logs the per-device outcome.
11. on_fetch_run: if set, run the external program (shell-free; section 9).

The lock filehandle is held in a lexical for the whole of fetch_device, so
it releases automatically when the sub returns, by any path.

2.3.1 Sequence diagram -- one device through fetch_device
---------------------------------------------------------

Time runs downward. --> is a return. A model's do_fetch is the only box
that talks to the device; everything else is core. The three early guards
(system-dir, allowlist, lock) can each end the device early with a recorded
outcome.

     driver -> Detector::fetch_device -> model.do_fetch -> Mailer/FS
    
     fetch_device(dev_id):
       read options (dev_option)
       find_latest(dev_id) (if needed)    -- scan FS -> (dir,file); before the guards
       system-dir check (always)          -- reject -> record_result(skipped), return
       directory allowlist (if present)   -- off-list -> record_result(skipped), return
       acquire_device_lock (if lock=on)   -- locked after retries -> skipped, return
       fetch() -> do_fetch():
           login (print_secret), open_*_debug_file, show-config,
           dump_config() -> write backup (O_EXCL)
         -- fetch failed -> record_result(failed), return
       config_equal(latest, new):
           unchanged -> config_discard(new); record_result(success,unchanged);
                        purge_ancient(); on_fetch_run?; return
           changed   -> record_result(success,changed); purge_ancient();
                        on_fetch_run (shell-free); return  ($fh closes -> lock frees)

Under -P, the whole column from fetch_device down runs inside a forked
worker; the record_result payloads travel back to the parent through the
result sink (section 2.4), not shared memory.

2.4 Parallel fetch (-P N)
-------------------------

run_fetch_phase($parallel) runs devices concurrently when -P N is given
(N >= 1 worker processes). It relies on POSIX fork, so it is a no-op on
native Windows -- fetchconfig.pl forces -P 1 (sequential) there. The model
is fork-per-device-batch:

- The parent creates a private File::Temp work directory (0700, auto-cleaned).
- It forks up to N workers; each worker runs fetch_device for its assigned
  device and writes its stdout/stderr to <tmpdir>/<dev_id>.out/.err
  (created under umask(077)), and its record_result payload to a result
  sink the parent reads back.
- %email_config, the device table, and the result list live in the parent;
  each worker is an independent process, so per-package globals are isolated
  by the fork -- a worker cannot see another worker's state.
- The parent installs SIG{INT}/SIG{TERM} handlers that forward the signal
  to the workers, so a killed parent does not orphan them.

Implication for extenders: anything a worker produces that the parent
needs (a new per-device result field, a summary counter) must travel through
the result sink (the serialized record_result payload), not a package
variable -- a package variable set in a worker is lost when the worker exits.
Mailer::record_result already serialises every key you pass it, so new
result fields propagate automatically if you add them as record_result
arguments.

