3. Data structures
==================

All of these are file-scoped (my) package variables unless noted. "File" =
the .pm that declares them.

3.1 Detector.pm -- the device registry
--------------------------------------

- %model_table : label -> model object. Populated by
  Detector::register($mod) at startup (each model's .pm registers itself
  when loaded). label is the device-table name (cisco-ios, generic, ...).

- @dev_order : ordered list of dev_ids in device-table order. The
  sequential fetch phase iterates this; the maintenance modes use it to list
  devices deterministically.

- %dev_id_table : dev_id -> source line number. Used to detect and
  report a duplicate dev_id.

- %dev_info_table : dev_id -> a hashref describing the device:

      {
        model       => $mod,          # the model object (from %model_table)
        dev_host    => $dev_host,     # hostname or IP (2nd field of the device line)
        dev_opt_tab => $dev_opt_tab,  # the merged per-device option hash (see 3.2)
        file        => $file,         # source device-table file (for messages)
        num         => $num,          # source line number
        line        => $line,         # the raw source line (for messages, masked)
      }

- The directory-allowlist structures (populated from directory: lines):
  - %dir_allow_tag : $TAG -> { type => repository|template|fetch_run|lock, path
    => <abs path> }. The $TAG indirection a device value uses.
  - %dir_allow_path : normalised allowed path -> type, for membership tests.
  - $dir_allow_present : true once any directory: line has been seen (switches
    the allowlist from "off/opt-in" to "enforced").
  - @dir_allow_rows : ordered [type, tag, path] for --list-allowed-dirs.
  - @dir_allow_errors : [reason, ...] for each rejected directory: line; a
    non-empty list aborts the run before any fetch (section 9) and is what
    --list-allowed-dirs lints.
  - $dir_allow_fetch_run_none / $dir_allow_report_none : set by directory:
    fetch_run none / directory: report none (disable that capability).
  - %warned_unlisted_dir : dedup set for the "no allowlist entry" warning, reset
    per device-table load.

- @SYSTEM_DIRS : the list of directories (/bin, /etc, /usr/lib, ...)
  that are never acceptable as a repository/template/fetch_run directory,
  tested by _system_dir_path().

- $DEV_ID_RE = qr/^[\w.-]+$/ : the allowed dev_id character set.
  A dev_id is also rejected if it is only dots.

- $default_lock_dir : <canonical dir of fetchconfig.pl>/.lock, set by
  Detector::init($log, $lock_dir) from the driver.

- $lock_dir_failed : set if any device's lock dir could not be created
  or used; the driver turns this into exit 10 after the run.

3.2 $dev_opt_tab -- the per-device option hash
----------------------------------------------

The single most important structure for an extender. It is a flat hashref:
option name -> string value, built in phase 1 by merging the model's
default: options under the device line's own options (device wins). Every
option in section 4 appears here by its bare name (repository, pass, keep,
debug, lock, to, ...). Models and the pipeline read it only through
the Abstract accessors, never by direct hash access:

- dev_option($tab, $name) -- the effective value, with a $TAG expanded to
  its real path for directory-valued options, and with timeout-family options
  validated (a non-positive-integer timeout/fetch_timeout/banner_timeout/
  prompt_settle_ms is ignored so the documented default applies).
- dev_option_raw($tab, $name) -- the effective value with no $TAG
  expansion (used by the allowlist enforcement, which must see the raw value).
- dev_option_flag($tab, $name, $default) -- a boolean option
  (on/off), e.g. debug, lock, changes_only (where applicable).
- dev_option_posint($tab, $name, $default, $allow_zero) -- a validated
  non-negative/positive integer, e.g. lock_retry (0 allowed),
  lock_retry_time (>=1).

To add an option: decide whether it is directory-valued (add it to
%DIR_VALUED_OPTION in Abstract so $TAG expands), numeric (read with
dev_option_posint), boolean (dev_option_flag), or plain (dev_option).
No parse change is needed -- parse_options accepts any key=value; the
option "exists" as soon as something reads it.

3.3 Mailer.pm -- the result record
----------------------------------

- @results : one hashref per device outcome, pushed by
  record_result(%args). The fields (every one is also written to the
  .status file and, under -P, serialised through the result sink):

      {
        dev_id     => ...,   # device id
        dev_host   => ...,   # hostname/IP
        ts         => ...,   # epoch of the fetch start (for Date/Time columns)
        elapsed    => ...,   # seconds the fetch took
        success    => 0|1,   # hard success/failure (undef on a 'skipped' row)
        size       => ...,   # backup size in bytes, or undef
        changed    => 'changed'|'unchanged'|'initial'|'n/a',
        state      => 'skipped' (optional; explicit state wins over success)
        repository => ...,   # so the per-device .status file can be written
        to         => ...,   # device-specific recipient override (the `to=` option)
      }

  The effective State shown in the mail is computed by row_state($row):
  an explicit state (currently only 'skipped') wins; otherwise
  success ? 'success' : 'failed'.

- %email_config : the parsed email: options (from, to, smtp,
  user, password, tls, report_dir, write_report, email_max_diff,
  web_report_url, report_logo, report_days, mask_secrets, footer).

- $rep_url, $rep_email_diffs, $rep_max_diff, $rep_mask : material
  carried from send_summaries into the HTML/text builders (the report link,
  the diffs to embed, the diff cap, the masking flag).

- $MAIL_LOGO_B64 : the inline e-mail header logo (base64 PNG), embedded
  as a multipart/related Content-ID: <fcwlogo> part. Independent of the
  on-disk report's report_logo=.

3.4 On-disk structures
----------------------

- Backup file : <repository>/<dev_id>/<dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS[tz]
  (optionally plus filename_append_suffix). parse_backup_filename() and
  sort_backups_desc() parse and order these by the parsed timestamp
  YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS, never by a plain string sort of the whole name (so the
  timezone token and a suffix cannot disturb the order). Written with
  O_EXCL so two runs completing in the same second cannot silently
  overwrite one another (section 9).

- .status file : <repository>/.<dev_id>.status -- the last-run result
  for that device (written atomically via a dotfile temp + rename), consumed
  by fetchconfig-web and the maintenance modes.

- .debug file : <repository>/<dev_id>.debug -- the full session trace
  when debug=on (0600; section 6). Overwritten each run.

- Lock file : <lock_dir>/<sha256(dev_id)>.lock -- a flock holder, never
  unlinked on release (section 8).

