5. Models and the model API
===========================

5.1 Lifecycle
-------------

Each model is a subclass of fetchconfig::model::Abstract. At use-time it
constructs one instance and calls Detector->register($self), which puts it
in %model_table under $self->label. The driver loads all models via
Detector at startup.

Per device, Detector::fetch_device calls $mod->fetch(...), whose default
implementation in Abstract delegates to the model's do_fetch(...). A model
therefore implements do_fetch (and label), and overrides prompt/login
hooks as needed; everything else is inherited.

5.2 The shared API (Abstract.pm)
--------------------------------

The methods a model (or an extender) relies on. Signatures are indicative;
read Abstract.pm for exact argument lists.

Session / transport
- ssh_open($dev_host, $dev_user, $dev_pass, $dev_timeout, $debug_fh,
  $dev_opt_tab) -- open a Net::OpenSSH session (SSH models). Host-key policy is
  set here (section 9, deliberate non-mitigation).
- open_ssh_debug_file(...) / open_telnet_debug_file($t, $dev_opt_tab, $repo,
  $dev_id, $dev_host) -- when debug=on, open <dev_id>.debug (0600) and attach it
  as the SSH/telnet session log. The telnet helper (9.71) is what gives every
  telnet model debug=; call it right after the connection opens and close() the
  returned handle at session end.
- wait_for_command_prompt(...), expect_enable_prompt(...),
  expect_enable_prompt_paging_auto(...), drain_until_idle(...),
  chat_banner(...), chat_show_conf(...) -- prompt and login helpers. Models
  override prompt_head() / prompt_tail() / interrupt_screens() /
  ssh_extra_opts() to tune behaviour.

Backup write / compare / expire
- dump_config($dev_id, $dev_opt_tab, \@lines) -- write the backup
  (O_EXCL, umask-driven perms, repository dir auto-created), returns
  ($dir, $file).
- find_latest($dev_id, $dev_opt_tab) -- locate the most recent prior backup.
- config_equal(...) / config_equal_ignoring_lines(...) -- compare two
  configs, ignoring volatile lines.
- config_discard($dir, $file) -- remove the just-written backup when it
  equals the previous one, and prune the dir if emptied.
- purge_ancient($dev_id, $dev_opt_tab) -- delete backups beyond keep=N
  (validates keep; section 9).

Options / secrets / text
- dev_option* accessors (section 3.2).
- print_secret($t, $secret) -- send a password/secret to the device **without
  logging it** (suspends the debug log around the send, writes a masked
  marker). Every model sends credentials through this; a new model MUST too.
- mask_secrets($line) -- redact pass=/enable=/community=/password=/
  secret=/key=/token=-style values before echoing a device-table line
  to the log.
- stripansi($s), regexp_quote_keep_bytes($s), escape_brackets($s),
  dequote_value($s) -- text helpers (ANSI stripping, byte-preserving regex
  quoting for learned prompts, etc.).

Locking
- lock_dir_resolve, device_lock_path, acquire_device_lock (section 8).

5.2.1 Call graph -- core <-> model <-> Abstract
-----------------------------------------------

How control flows into a model and what the model calls back. ( ) = a hook
the model overrides; [ ] = a shared helper the model calls. Arrows point
from caller to callee.

     Detector::fetch_device
       -> Abstract::fetch -> <Model>::do_fetch   (model implements do_fetch)
    
     <Model>::do_fetch calls shared helpers [ ] and model hooks ( ):
       [ssh_open] / Net::Telnet->new         open transport
       [open_ssh_debug_file]/[open_telnet_debug_file]   attach .debug (debug=on)
       (prompt_head)(prompt_tail)(interrupt_screens)    model-tuned prompts/pager
       [wait_for_command_prompt][expect_enable_prompt]
       [chat_banner][chat_login][chat_show_conf]        login/show
       [print_secret]                        send EVERY credential here
       [stripansi][regexp_quote_keep_bytes][escape_brackets]
       [dev_option][dev_option_flag][dev_option_raw][dev_option_posint]
       [dump_config]                         write the backup (O_EXCL)
       => returns (config_dir, config_file) or undef
    
     Core calls Abstract directly (find_latest runs before the fetch):
       [find_latest] [config_equal]/[config_equal_ignoring_lines]
       [config_discard] [purge_ancient]
       [lock_dir_resolve][device_lock_path][acquire_device_lock]
    
     Model overrides (defaults): label() do_fetch() prompt_head('')
       prompt_tail('#$') interrupt_screens(()) ssh_extra_opts(())

Rule of thumb: a model implements label + do_fetch and overrides
only the prompt/transport hooks it needs; it calls the [ ] helpers for
everything else and never reimplements them. These core-driven helpers --
config_equal, config_discard, purge_ancient, locking, and find_latest
(which core calls before the fetch) -- run in core, not the model; a model
just returns the written path.

5.3 Adding a model
------------------

Follow How-To-add-a-model (the authoritative recipe) together with the
data-flow context in section 2.3/section 5.2. In short: create
fetchconfig/model/Yours.pm
as an Abstract subclass, implement label and do_fetch, send credentials
with print_secret, call open_telnet_debug_file/open_ssh_debug_file so
debug= works, write the config with dump_config, and register the model.
Add it to t/00-compile.t's expectations and the per-model matrix in the
HTML docs. Capture a real session first with tools/record-session.pl
(README.record-session).

For a device that speaks a config-template rather than a bespoke dialect,
prefer the generic model and a template -- see README.generic-model and
README.template_engine; no Perl is needed.

------------------------------------------------------------

6. Debug tracing (debug=on)
===========================

debug=on writes a 0600 <repository>/<dev_id>.debug session trace, available
for every model (since 9.71). Three mechanisms, all producing the same
file contract:

- SSH models -> open_ssh_debug_file (records the ssh -v trace +
  session).
- Telnet models -> open_telnet_debug_file (attaches the Net::Telnet
  dump_log).
- HTTP / SNMP models -> their own implementation (HTTP request/response;
  SNMP operations + TFTP transfer).

Credentials never reach the log because models send them via print_secret,
which suspends the log around the send. The file still contains configuration
secrets (hashes, community strings), so it is 0600 and should be deleted after
troubleshooting.

------------------------------------------------------------

7. Maintenance / lookup modes (Tools.pm)
========================================

These are standalone modes: they use phase-1 registration, operate on the
stored backups (never fetch), and exit. Summary (see README command-line
reference for exact behaviour):

Flag                    Purpose
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
-g dev                  print the latest backup of dev to stdout (-f file to
                        write it).
-l dev                  list the backups of dev.
-z dev / -Z             find zero-byte backups for one device / all devices; -D
                        also deletes the files, -T previews.
-o / -e                 orphaned-device / empty-directory report; -D deletes, -T
                        previews.
-s dev / -S             suffix check for one / all devices.
--check-template PATH   structural check of a generic-model template; exits 0 ok
                        / 1 errors / 2 file problem. No device table needed; no
                        allowlist applied.
--template-syntax PATH  print a template's # syntax_style: value for a config
                        viewer.
--list-allowed-dirs     print and lint the directory: allowlist.
--clean-lock-dir [N]    remove lock files older than N days (default 30, min 1);
                        -T previews; flock-first so a held lock is never
                        removed.

Exit codes of note: 0 success; 2 usage/allowlist-abort/bad-argument;
10 lock directory unusable; 255 fatal die; lookup modes also use 1
(e.g. zero-byte/orphan found).

------------------------------------------------------------

8. Locking
==========

lock=on serialises concurrent backups of the same device (overlapping cron,
or a web "Backup Now" over a cron run). Mechanism:

- Key: sha256_hex(dev_id); path: <lock_dir>/<hash>.lock.
- Lock dir: lock_dir= (a directory: lock $TAG or a path), else the
  default <dir of fetchconfig.pl>/.lock, created 0700. If it cannot be
  created/used, the run exits 10 at the end.
- Acquire: sysopen(..., O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW, 0600) then
  flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB). On contention, retry lock_retry times (default
  5; 0 = skip immediately) at lock_retry_time-second intervals (default 10),
  then record the device as skipped and continue. The process exit code
  is not changed by a lock-skip.
- Release: automatic when the holding process exits (even on crash). The
  lock file is never unlinked on release (unlinking would open a
  two-holders race). --clean-lock-dir removes stale files flock-first.

Works across the forked -P workers and across separate invocations. Requires
a local filesystem (flock over NFS on AIX is unreliable) -- a documented
operator responsibility.

------------------------------------------------------------

9. Security mechanisms
======================

9.1 Trust model (read this first)
---------------------------------

- The device table is the credential store and a code-execution surface.
  Whoever can write it already holds every device password and, via
  on_fetch_run, can run a program as the fetchconfig user. The security
  mechanisms below defend against content mistakes and external attackers,
  not against a hostile table author -- that is out of scope by construction.
- fetchconfig-web split. In the fetchconfig-web deployment, content is
  read as the unprivileged nobody user (-l/-g) and backups are written
  as root via sudo (-g/-o). This is why repository files/directories are
  deliberately umask-readable, not owner-only (see 9.6) -- an unprivileged
  reader must be able to read them.

9.2 Credential handling
-----------------------

- print_secret sends passwords to the device without logging them
  (suspends the session log around the send, leaves a masked marker). Every
  model uses it.
- mask_secrets (Abstract, for device-table lines echoed to the log;
  Detector has a matching one) redacts pass=/enable=/community=/
  password=/passphrase=/secret=/key=/token=-prefixed values, quoted
  values included, so a malformed table line in an error message does not leak
  a credential.
- mask_line (Report, for config content in the HTML report and the
  mailed diffs) redacts secret N <hash> / password N <hash> forms. It is
  best-effort and biases toward masking (a false positive hides a non-secret
  in the diff view; never the reverse).

9.3 Command execution -- on_fetch_run
-------------------------------------

on_fetch_run is executed without a shell: the configured value is split
into argv with Text::ParseWords::shellwords and run with the list form of
system (system { $argv[0] } @argv), which goes to execvp() directly.
Shell metacharacters (;, |, $(...), backticks, >) are passed to the
program as literal text, not interpreted. The program must not live in a
system directory. A start failure, signal, or non-zero exit is logged.

9.4 Path safety
---------------

- dev_id whitelist ^[\w.-]+$ (dots-only rejected) -- prevents a device
  id from escaping the repository via ../.
- Directory allowlist -- when a directory: section exists, repository=,
  template_dir=, lock_dir=, and the on_fetch_run program directory must
  resolve under an allowed path; paths are checked for /../, /./, absolute
  form, and legal characters; a $TAG is expanded. Off-list -> device skipped.
- System-directory rejection (_system_dir_path) -- those four directory
  uses may never be /bin, /etc, /usr/lib, ... or the root, with or without
  an allowlist. A directory: line that declares one is rejected at parse
  time.
- Abort on a broken allowlist -- if any directory: line failed to parse,
  the run aborts (exit 2) before any fetch, rather than continuing with a
  silently reduced allowlist (fail-closed).
- O_EXCL backup write -- dump_config creates the backup exclusively, so
  two runs finishing in the same one-second filename window cannot silently
  overwrite each other (the second is refused and reported).

9.5 Locking
-----------

See section 8 -- O_NOFOLLOW on the lock file, 0600 lock files, 0700 lock dir,
flock-based, never-unlink-on-release, flock-first cleanup.

9.6 File permissions
--------------------

Backup files and their directories, and the .status file, are created with
the process umask -- deliberately not forced owner-only, because the
fetchconfig-web nobody reader must read them (9.1). Restrict the repository
directory yourself if the host is shared. The .debug file is 0600
(it can contain secrets even though passwords are masked). Parallel-fetch
worker scratch files are owner-only (they live in a private temp dir).

9.7 Mail
--------

- SMTP header-injection -- from, subject, and every recipient have
  CR/LF stripped before the headers are written.
- No plaintext fallback -- tls=starttls|ssl never silently downgrades to
  an unencrypted send; if TLS fails the mail is skipped and logged.

9.8 Deliberate NON-mitigations (do not "fix" these blindly)
-----------------------------------------------------------

- SSH host-key verification is OFF (StrictHostKeyChecking=no,
  UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null). This is a known, deferred exposure
  (audit finding F3) traded off against switch-key churn on hardware swaps.
  A design for TOFU verification plus a --forget-host operator command is
  specified in fetchconfig-F3-ssh-hostkey-plan.md for when it is addressed.
  Turning verification on without that operator tooling would break the
  hardware-swap workflow.
- Repository is umask-readable by design (9.6) -- not a permissions bug.

------------------------------------------------------------

10. Notification subsystem (Mailer / Report)
============================================

- send_summaries builds @global_rows from @results and sends the
  global summary to the email: to= recipient, then groups devices by their
  to= override and sends each distinct override recipient one mail with
  only its own devices (dedup). The global recipient is never double-mailed.
- The mail is multipart: a multipart/related wrapping a
  multipart/alternative (a plain-text summary built by build_report_text
  -- the stats and device tables in text, no diffs -- and an HTML part built by
  build_report_html), plus the inline logo image. The MIME emission matches
  the fetchconfig-web mailer byte-structure (folded boundaries, CRLF, 12-byte
  random boundaries) so the inline logo renders.
- web_report_url is the base fetchconfig-web CGI URL; the mailer appends
  ?action=view_report&file=<basename> to build the "full report" button
  link.
- Report.pm writes the on-disk HTML change report (a summary table plus
  a coloured, masked diff per changed device) to the report directory; the
  mail embeds up to email_max_diff diffs and links to the full report.

